<DemoExample Title="Basic" RazorCode="@example1RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example1CsharpCode" Id="example1">
    <div>
        Each item of the navbar renders an icon over its text and becomes a link when it carries a <b>Url</b>.
        A custom item type is read through <b>NameSelectors</b>, which maps each field of the navbar onto a
        property of that type. By default the navbar runs in the <b>Automatic</b> mode, where it highlights the
        item whose URL points at the current page all by itself.
    </div>
    <br /><br />
    <div class="container">
        <BitNavBar Items="basicNavBarCustoms"
                   NameSelectors="@(new() { Text = { Selector = item => item.Title },
                                            IconName = { Selector = item => item.ImageName } })" />
    </div>
</DemoExample>

<DemoExample Title="Disabled" RazorCode="@example2RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example2CsharpCode" Id="example2" PreventRenderForMcpClient>
    <div>
        <b>IsEnabled</b> turns the whole navbar off, and the <b>IsEnabled</b> selector turns a single item off.
        A disabled item is not only greyed out: it loses its link, its tab stop and its keyboard stop, so it
        cannot be reached or followed at all.
    </div>
    <br /><br />
    <div>Disabled:</div><br />
    <div class="container">
        <BitNavBar Items="basicNavBarCustoms" IsEnabled="false"
                   NameSelectors="@(new() { Text = { Selector = item => item.Title },
                                            IconName = { Selector = item => item.ImageName } })" />
    </div>
    <br /><br /><br /><br />
    <div>Disabled item:</div><br />
    <div class="container">
        <BitNavBar Items="basicNavBarCustomsDisabled"
                   NameSelectors="@(new() { Text = { Selector = item => item.Title },
                                            IconName = { Selector = item => item.ImageName },
                                            IsEnabled = { Selector = item => item.Disabled is false } })" />
    </div>
</DemoExample>

<DemoExample Title="Manual Mode" RazorCode="@example3RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example3CsharpCode" Id="example3" PreventRenderForMcpClient>
    <div>
        In the <b>Manual</b> mode the selection is driven by clicks instead of by the current URL, which is
        what a navbar that switches between panels of the same page needs. <b>DefaultSelectedItem</b> sets the
        item to start on. A navbar that switches panels rather than pages marks its current item with an
        <b>AriaCurrent</b> of <b>True</b>, since it is not the page that is current but one item of a set.
    </div>
    <br /><br />
    <div class="container">
        <BitNavBar Mode="BitNavMode.Manual"
                   Items="basicNavBarCustoms"
                   DefaultSelectedItem="basicNavBarCustoms[0]"
                   NameSelectors="@(new() { Text = { Selector = item => item.Title },
                                            IconName = { Selector = item => item.ImageName } })" />
    </div>
</DemoExample>

<DemoExample Title="URL Matching" RazorCode="@example4RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example4CsharpCode" Id="example4" PreventRenderForMcpClient>
    <div>
        In the <b>Automatic</b> mode the navbar selects the item whose URL points at the current page.
        <b>Match</b> sets how those URLs are compared for the whole navbar, and the <b>Match</b> of an item
        overrides it: <b>Exact</b> asks for the same URL, <b>Prefix</b> also matches everything below it,
        <b>Wildcard</b> reads the URL of the item as a pattern in which <b>?</b> stands for a single character
        other than <b>/</b>, <b>*</b> for any number of them and <b>**</b> for any number of characters at all,
        slashes included, and <b>Regex</b> reads it as a regular expression. <b>Exact</b> and <b>Prefix</b>
        compare the path alone when the URL of the item carries neither a query string nor a fragment, and the
        whole URL when it carries one, and they ignore the letter case and a trailing slash either way; a
        <b>Wildcard</b> or a <b>Regex</b> pattern is run against both, so it can match the query and the
        fragment as well. <b>AdditionalUrls</b> gives an item other URLs to light up on. The navbars below are
        all matched against the URL of this page.
    </div>
    <br /><br />
    <div>Exact (the default):</div><br />
    <div class="container">
        <BitNavBar Items="exactMatchCustoms"
                   NameSelectors="@(new() { Text = { Selector = item => item.Title },
                                            IconName = { Selector = item => item.ImageName },
                                            Url = { Selector = item => item.Link } })" />
    </div>
    <br /><br /><br /><br />
    <div>Prefix:</div><br />
    <div class="container">
        <BitNavBar Items="prefixMatchCustoms" Match="BitNavMatch.Prefix"
                   NameSelectors="@(new() { Text = { Selector = item => item.Title },
                                            IconName = { Selector = item => item.ImageName },
                                            Url = { Selector = item => item.Link } })" />
    </div>
    <br /><br /><br /><br />
    <div>Wildcard:</div><br />
    <div class="container">
        <BitNavBar Items="wildcardMatchCustoms"
                   NameSelectors="@(new() { Text = { Selector = item => item.Title },
                                            IconName = { Selector = item => item.ImageName },
                                            Url = { Selector = item => item.Link },
                                            Match = { Selector = item => item.Matching },
                                            IsEnabled = { Selector = item => item.Disabled is false } })" />
    </div>
    <br /><br /><br /><br />
    <div>Regex:</div><br />
    <div class="container">
        <BitNavBar Items="regexMatchCustoms"
                   NameSelectors="@(new() { Text = { Selector = item => item.Title },
                                            IconName = { Selector = item => item.ImageName },
                                            Url = { Selector = item => item.Link },
                                            Match = { Selector = item => item.Matching },
                                            IsEnabled = { Selector = item => item.Disabled is false } })" />
    </div>
    <br /><br /><br /><br />
    <div>AdditionalUrls:</div><br />
    <div class="container">
        <BitNavBar Items="additionalUrlsCustoms"
                   NameSelectors="@(new() { Text = { Selector = item => item.Title },
                                            IconName = { Selector = item => item.ImageName },
                                            Url = { Selector = item => item.Link },
                                            AdditionalUrls = { Selector = item => item.ExtraLinks } })" />
    </div>
</DemoExample>

<DemoExample Title="IconOnly" RazorCode="@example5RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example5CsharpCode" Id="example5" PreventRenderForMcpClient>
    <div>
        <b>IconOnly</b> drops the text of every item and keeps the icons alone. The text is still what names
        the item for a screen reader, and it is moved onto the item as its label and its tooltip, so nothing
        is lost by hiding it.
    </div>
    <br /><br />
    <div class="container">
        <BitNavBar IconOnly
                   Items="basicNavBarCustoms"
                   NameSelectors="@(new() { Text = { Selector = item => item.Title },
                                            IconName = { Selector = item => item.ImageName } })" />
    </div>
</DemoExample>

<DemoExample Title="HideUnselectedText" RazorCode="@example6RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example6CsharpCode" Id="example6" PreventRenderForMcpClient>
    <div>
        <b>HideUnselectedText</b> keeps the text of the selected item alone and leaves the rest with their
        icon, which is how a bar holding more destinations stays readable without dropping every label.
        Select an item to move the label along with the selection.
    </div>
    <br /><br />
    <div class="container">
        <BitNavBar HideUnselectedText
                   Mode="BitNavMode.Manual"
                   Items="basicNavBarCustoms"
                   DefaultSelectedItem="basicNavBarCustoms[0]"
                   NameSelectors="@(new() { Text = { Selector = item => item.Title },
                                            IconName = { Selector = item => item.ImageName } })" />
    </div>
</DemoExample>

<DemoExample Title="Layout" RazorCode="@example7RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example7CsharpCode" Id="example7" PreventRenderForMcpClient>
    <div>
        <b>InlineText</b> puts the text of an item beside its icon instead of under it, and <b>Vertical</b>
        stacks the items in a column, which turns the navbar into a navigation rail. The two combine into a
        classic side rail.
    </div>
    <br /><br />
    <div>InlineText:</div><br />
    <div class="container">
        <BitNavBar InlineText
                   Items="basicNavBarCustoms"
                   NameSelectors="@(new() { Text = { Selector = item => item.Title },
                                            IconName = { Selector = item => item.ImageName } })" />
    </div>
    <br /><br /><br /><br />
    <div>Vertical:</div><br />
    <div class="container">
        <BitNavBar Vertical FitWidth
                   Items="basicNavBarCustoms"
                   NameSelectors="@(new() { Text = { Selector = item => item.Title },
                                            IconName = { Selector = item => item.ImageName } })" />
    </div>
    <br /><br /><br /><br />
    <div>Vertical &amp; InlineText:</div><br />
    <div class="container">
        <BitNavBar Vertical InlineText FitWidth
                   Items="basicNavBarCustoms"
                   NameSelectors="@(new() { Text = { Selector = item => item.Title },
                                            IconName = { Selector = item => item.ImageName } })" />
    </div>
</DemoExample>

<DemoExample Title="Width" RazorCode="@example8RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example8CsharpCode" Id="example8" PreventRenderForMcpClient>
    <div>
        <b>FitWidth</b> shrinks the navbar to the width of its own content, and <b>FullWidth</b> stretches it
        over the full width of its container. Without either one the navbar takes the width its container
        gives it. Those two size the bar; <b>Justified</b> sizes the items inside it, giving each one an equal
        share instead of the width of its own label, which is how a navigation bar keeps its destinations on
        a predictable grid.
    </div>
    <br /><br />
    <div>FitWidth:</div><br />
    <div class="container">
        <BitNavBar FitWidth
                   Items="basicNavBarCustoms"
                   NameSelectors="@(new() { Text = { Selector = item => item.Title },
                                            IconName = { Selector = item => item.ImageName } })" />
    </div>
    <br /><br /><br /><br />
    <div>FullWidth:</div><br />
    <div class="container">
        <BitNavBar FullWidth
                   Items="basicNavBarCustoms"
                   NameSelectors="@(new() { Text = { Selector = item => item.Title },
                                            IconName = { Selector = item => item.ImageName } })" />
    </div>
    <br /><br /><br /><br />
    <div>Justified (items of unequal label length):</div><br />
    <div class="container">
        <BitNavBar Items="unevenNavBarCustoms"
                   NameSelectors="@(new() { Text = { Selector = item => item.Title },
                                            IconName = { Selector = item => item.ImageName } })" />
    </div>
    <br /><br />
    <div class="container">
        <BitNavBar Justified
                   Items="unevenNavBarCustoms"
                   NameSelectors="@(new() { Text = { Selector = item => item.Title },
                                            IconName = { Selector = item => item.ImageName } })" />
    </div>
</DemoExample>

<DemoExample Title="Badge" RazorCode="@example9RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example9CsharpCode" Id="example9" PreventRenderForMcpClient>
    <div>
        <b>Badge</b> puts a short count or status on the icon of an item, and <b>Dot</b> marks it as needing
        attention without a number. Both are drawn on the icon and hidden from assistive technology, and the
        badge is folded into the name of the item instead, so it is announced once rather than twice
        &mdash; "Inbox (12)". <b>BadgeAriaLabel</b> replaces that with a description a screen reader can read
        out on its own, and is the only way a <b>Dot</b> is announced at all, since a dot carries no text.
    </div>
    <br /><br />
    <div class="container">
        <BitNavBar Items="badgeNavBarCustoms"
                   NameSelectors="@(new() { Text = { Selector = item => item.Title },
                                            IconName = { Selector = item => item.ImageName },
                                            Badge = { Selector = item => item.Counter },
                                            BadgeAriaLabel = { Selector = item => item.CounterLabel },
                                            Dot = { Selector = item => item.Marker } })" />
    </div>
</DemoExample>

<DemoExample Title="Accent" RazorCode="@example10RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example10CsharpCode" Id="example10" PreventRenderForMcpClient>
    <div>
        <b>Accent</b> fills the hovered and the selected item with a color, which gives the selection an
        indicator of its own instead of leaving it to the text color alone. A semantic accent moves the whole
        content of the item onto its on-color, while a surface accent only tints the background.
    </div>
